# {{app-name}} - Flows

## Overview

{{app-name}} is a collaborative platform that allows users to manage and share shopping lists.\
{{app-name}} also offers a B2B service, providing statistics and insights based on real user data

To serve both individual and business needs, {{app-name}} is organized into two segments:

1. **B2C** - The consumer-facing part of {{app-name}}.
2. **B2B** - Provides analytics and insights derived from user activity.

Respectively, the flows below are organized into those categories too.

{{space}}

## Notes

Before continue, make sure you read the following notes:

> [!NOTE]
>
> **All events are logged in {{kafka}}**
>
> Each event has `type`, `data` and `state` to them.\
> When failing service is reinitiated, it will look over all of the `"state": "started"` events, and retry them.
>
> For brevity, kafka logs are omitted from flow diagrams unless it plays a special role beyond standard logging.

> [!NOTE]
>
> **From {{redis}} to {{db}}**
>
> All writes on {{redis}} are followed with notifications in {{kafka}} for the {{db-writer-service}} to push update
>
> For brevity, update {{db}} logs are omitted from flow diagram unless it plays a special role beyond standard role.\
> See [{{db-writer-service}}](#db-writer-service) for implementation details.

> [!NOTE]
>
> **Scattered flows!**
>
> Some flows are distributed across multiple services because they are part of larger, overarching flows.\
> If you can’t find a sub-flow in one place, try searching within the larger flows it may belong to.

> [!NOTE]
>
> By default, {{get-snippet}} requests return the full data.\
> When tailored response is required, the flow defines a response template.

> [!NOTE]
>
> For best visibility, open this MD file through Visual Studio Code, using the [Markdown Preview Enhanced](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=shd101wyy.markdown-preview-enhanced)

{{space}}

## Architecture

![ShopList Architecture v1.1](../../Architecture/Current/ShopList-Architecture-v1.1.drawio.svg)

{{space}}

## 1. B2C Flows

This part is organized into 3 categories:

1. **Base App** - consisting of `{{auth-service}}`, `{{users-service}}`, `{{lists-service}}`, `{{chat-service}}`, `{{db-writer-service}}`
2. **Message Process** - `{{message-analyzer-service}}`, `{{keyword-manager-service}}`, `{{scoring-service}}`
3. **AI Suggestions** - `{{ai-suggestions}}`, `{{product-history-manager}}`

> [!NOTE]
>
> **All {{rest}} request must have JWT**
>
> All {{rest}} API requests pass through {{nginx}}, which sends them to the {{auth-service}} for JWT token validation.\
> Valid requests are modified to have `user_id` instead of JWT before forwarding to downstream services.
>
> For brevity, {{nginx}} is omitted from flow diagrams unless it plays a service-specific role beyond standard authentication.
>
> See [{{auth-service}}: JWT to `user_id` Flow](#jwt-to-userid) for implementation details.

{{space}}

## 1.1. Base App

### {{auth-service}}

Manages Login and JWTs

1. **Login**
   1. {{front}} redirect to {{auth0}}
      {{↓}}
   2. User Login
      {{↓}}
   3. JWT is sent to the {{auth-service}}
      {{↓}}
   - [ ] ==_TODO: FIGURE OUT WHATS NEXT_==

{{space}}

2. **JWT to `user_id`**<span id="jwt-to-userid"></span>
   1. {{front}} sends {{rest-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. {{nginx}} extracts JWT
      {{↓}}
   3. {{auth-service}} validates JWT, returns `user_id`
      {{↓}}
   4. {{nginx}} adds `user_id` to headers
      {{↓}}
   5. Destination service receives request

{{space}}

### {{users-service}}

Manages users, user data, user settings

> [!NOTE]
>
> This service will be divided into Users Manager and Settings Service, when more settings will be added.

> [!NOTE]
>
> As {{auth-service}} is yet to be realized, flows might change in the future.

1. **Get Users / Setting**
   1. {{front}} sends a {{get-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. {{users-service}} fetches users details from {{redis}}.
      {{↓}}
   3. If type of detail is stored in {{auth0}}, {{auth-service}} fetches them.
      {{↓}}
   4. {{front}} receives users details in a list

{{space}}

2. **Get User by {{user-id}}**
   1. {{front}} sends a {{get-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. {{users-service}} fetches user details from {{redis}}.
      {{↓}}
   3. {{front}} receives users details in an object

{{space}}

3. **Update user / users / settings / setting**

   > [!NOTE]
   >
   > No setting is sent through the {{ws-service}} to others.\
   > Updates in settings that are affecting others, for example `seen`, will affect others after they will do a new {{rest-snippet}} request
   1. {{front}} sends a {{put-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. {{users-service}} update user details in {{redis}}.
      {{↓}}
   3. {{db-writer-service}} updates details in {{db}} {{async-operation}}
      {{↓}}
   4. {{auth-service}} updates details in {{auth0}}
      {{↓}}
   5. {{front}} receives users details in an object

{{space}}

4. **Send connection status through {{ws-service}}**

   > [!NOTE]
   >
   > This is part 1 of the combined flow for getting user status
   1. When user enters the app, the {{front}} receives all user details (see flow above).
      {{↓}}
   2. If allowed, and when {{ws-connection}} is open, {{front}} sends a {{ws-message}} that the user's connected
      {{↓}}
   3. {{ws-service}} broadcast the message through all of the rooms the user is part of
      {{↓}}
   4. {{front}} receives message, and ignores it unless the user is currently displayed in the chat settings

{{space}}

5. **Get User connection status**

   This flow is part of a bigger flow of getting list settings.\
   See [{{lists-service}} flows](#list-settings) for details

{{space}}

6.  **Remove User**
    - **What will be removed:**
      1. All personal details, settings, and unshared lists.
      2. User data from {{auth0}}

    - **What will stay:**
      1. {{user-id}} (and {{removed}} toggle will be on)
      2. All of the shared lists, closed or open. (Creator will be displayed as {{removed_account}})
      3. Every mention of the user will display {{removed_account}}\
         {{space}}
    1.  {{front}} sends a {{delete-snippet}} request
        {{↓}}
    2.  {{users-service}} removes all of the details about the user, except it's {{user-id}} from {{redis}}.\
        Toggles the {{removed}} value to {{true}}
        {{↓}}
    3.  If GDPR allows, stores single value for later reconnect the user to existing data.\
        {{users-service}} fetches this data from {{auth0}}
    - [ ] ==_TODO: Check GDPR Guides_==
          {{↓}}
    4. {{auth-service}} removes user from {{auth0}}
       {{↓}}
    5. {{lists-service}} removes all of the associated lists that were not shared, including all associated items from {{redis}}.
       {{↓}}
    6. {{chat-service}} removes all of the associated chats, including the messages there were sent, if any, from {{redis}}.\
       (All of the messages sent by the removed user that are not part of unshared lists are saved though).
       {{↓}}
    7. {{product-history-manager}} removes the list of latest items from {{redis}}
       {{↓}}
    8. {{ws-service}} removes the user from all of the rooms he's in.
       {{↓}}
    9. {{users-service}} returns an `operation succeeded` result to the {{front}}
       {{↓}}
    10. {{front}} display the Sign Up page.

{{space}}

7. **Logout**
   1. {{front}} removes identification details from localhost
      {{↓}}
   2. {{ws-service}} removes the user from all of his rooms
      {{↓}}
   - [ ] ==_TODO: FIGURE OUT WHATS NEXT_==
         {{↓}}
   3. {{front}} displays Login Screen

{{space}}

### {{lists-service}}

1. **Get Lists** - _SSE_

   This flow is used in the Home Page and focused on minimal metadata and preview-display specific data
   1. {{front}} sends {{get-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. Lists are fetched from {{redis}}.
      {{↓}}
   3. For every list, items are fetched from {{redis}} - 5 at most.
      {{↓}}
   4. Lists Service publishes event to Kafka, consumed by {{chat-service}}
      {{↓}}
   5. **First response is sent to {{front}}.**\
      _Content of Response:_

   ```JSON
   [
      {
         "id": "",
         "name": "",
         "update time": "",
         "silent": true,
         "items": [
            {
               "id": "",
               "content": "",
               "checked": false
            }
         ]
      }
   ]
   ```

   6. Displayed in {{front}}
      ***
   7. {{chat-service}} read event from {{kafka}}
      {{↓}}
   8. {{chat-service}} fetches amount of unread messages for every list, and last message
      {{↓}}
   9. **Second response is sent to the front.**\
      _Content of Response:_

   ```JSON
   [
      {
         "id": "",
         "unread_messages": 0,
         "last_message": ""
      }
   ]
   ```

   10. {{front}} display new data.
       {{↓}}
   11. SSE Closes.\
       _Later updates are based on WS activities._

{{space}}

2. **Get List by ID**
   1. {{front}} sends {{get-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. List is fetched from {{redis}} based on ID.
      {{↓}}
   3. List returns to {{front}}, with all metadata and items metadata.
      {{↓}}
   4. {{front}} displays list

{{space}}

3. **Create new list**
   1. {{front}} sends a {{post-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. {{lists-service}} creates a new empty list in {{redis}}
      {{↓}}
   3. {{chat-service}} creates a new chat room
      {{↓}}
   4. {{ws-service}} enters the user to associated room
      {{↓}}
   5. {{front}} displays the new list

{{space}}

4. **Update List / Item / List Setting**
   1. {{front}} sends {{post-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. List / Item is updated on {{redis}}.\
      **For items** - when version number is lower than what's on {{redis}}, new item is created instead of modification of existing item.
      {{↓}}
   3. {{ws-service}} fetches the event from {{kafka}}
      {{↓}}
   4. Update is broadcasted to all room members
      {{↓}}
   5. {{front}} displays update.
      > [!NOTE]
      >
      > For the user who made the update, it displayed before being broadcasted. If there is a problem, silent retry and backup in localhost.

{{space}}

5. **Share List**

   > [!NOTE]
   > **SMS not part of MVP**
   >
   > Apart from mentioning the SMS Service, the current flow does not include the SMS path.
   1. {{front}} sends {{post-snippet}} request with list of {{user-id}} and unrecognized phone numbers
      {{↓}}
   2. {{lists-service}} adds members to list
      {{↓}}
   3. {{lists-service}} publishes event to {{kafka}}, consumed by:
      - {{chat-service}}
      - {{sms-service}}

      {{_↓}}

   4. {{chat-service}} enters users to equivalent chat
      {{↓}}
   5. {{ws-service}} adds users to room
      {{↓}}
   6. {{lists-service}} publish event to {{kafka}}, consumed by {{ws-service}} with following data:

   ```JSON
   {
      "user_id": [""],
      "list": {
         "id": "",
         "name": "",
         "update time": "",
         "silent": false,
         "items": [
            {
               "id": "",
               "content": "",
               "checked": false
            }
         ]
      }
   }
   ```

   7. {{ws-service}} consumes event from {{kafka}}, sends message to all users under `user_id`
      {{↓}}
   8. {{front}} displays new list

{{space}}

6. **Leave List**\
   Relevant only if the user is **NOT** the only member of the list
   1. {{front}} sends {{put-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. The {{lists-service}} removes the user from the list
      {{↓}}
   3. {{chat-service}} removes the user from the equivalent chat in {{redis}}
      {{↓}}
   4. {{ws-service}} removes the user from the equivalent room

{{space}}

7. **Remove List**\
   Relevant only if the user **IS THE ONLY MEMBER** of the list
   1. {{front}} sends {{delete-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. The {{lists-service}} removes the list and all of the items from {{redis}}
      {{↓}}
   3. {{chat-service}} removes chat and all of the message from {{redis}}
      {{↓}}
   4. {{db-writer-service}} updates {{db}} accordingly {{async-operation}}.
      {{↓}}
   5. {{ws-service}} removes the user from the equivalent room

{{space}}

8. **Close List**

   This is different from removing the list. It leaves the list as it was.
   1. {{front}} sends {{put-snippet}} request
      {{↓}}
   2. {{lists-service}} changes the `state` in the `list_id` to `closed`
      {{↓}}
   3. {{ws-service}} keeps the users in the room, but it stops accepting any {{ws-message}} that is not "list is not open anymore"

{{space}}

9. **Get list settings**<span id="list-settings"></span>
   1. {{front}} sends a {{get-snippet}} request with list of users with undefined connection status
      {{↓}}
   2. {{lists-service}} fetches data from {{redis}}
      {{↓}}
   3. {{users-service}} fetches connection status for each user in the list settings
      {{↓}}
   4. {{lists-service}} send response to front
      {{↓}}
   5. {{front}} displays data

{{space}}

### {{chat-service}}

1. **Send message**
   ![Flow Diagram](./Diagrams/Chat_Service_-_Send_Message.drawio.svg)
   1. {{front}} sends message through {{ws-connection}}
      {{↓}}
   2. {{ws-connection}} provides the message to the {{chat-service}}, while broadcasting it to all of the room members
      {{↓}}
   3. {{chat-service}} writes all of the messages in {{redis}}
      {{↓}}
   4. The {{front}} of all of the listeners receives the message, and ping the {{ws-service}}
      {{↓}}
   5. The {{ws-service}} provides the ping messages to the {{chat-service}}
      {{after-awhile}}
   6. For every user who sees the message (enters viewable area in {{front}}), {{front}} sends `seen` {{ws-message}}
      {{↓}}
   7. The {{ws-service}} provides the `seen` messages to {{chat-service}}
      {{after-awhile}}
   8. When {{chat-service}} sees that all of the listeners received the message,\
      it sends the sender a `received by all` {{ws-message}}.
      {{after-awhile}}
   9. When {{chat-service}} sees that all of the listeners saw the message,\
      it sends the sender a `saw by all` {{ws-message}}.

{{space}}

2. **Edit message**
   1. {{front}} sends new new message content through {{ws-connection}}
      {{↓}}
   2. {{ws-connection}} sends new content to all listeners and to {{chat-service}}
      {{↓}}
   3. {{chat-service}} stores new message content in {{redis}}
      {{after-awhile}}
   4. All listeners' {{front}} display new content

{{space}}

### {{db-writer-service}}

All Services are writing and reading from {{redis}}. Only the {{db-writer-service}} writes the {{db}}, and when {{redis}} is re-instantiate, this service puts relevant data in {{redis}}.

The {{db-writer-service}} updates the {{db}} every 30sec.\
For that reason, this is an async operation.

> [!NOTE]
>
> This service may not be part of MVP
